export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"carving","name":"Router carving","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"carving-start","scope":"addon:carving","title":"Router carving: getting started","summary":"What the Router carving add-on does, how to switch it on, and how to go from a drawing or a picture to G-code for a router in a few steps.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["carving","router","cnc","v-carve","vcarve","engraving","relief","depth map","g-code","gcode","sign","lettering","inlay","grbl","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-v-carve","text":"Your first V-carve","level":2},{"id":"always-air-cut-the-first-run","text":"Always air-cut the first run","level":2},{"id":"what-it-is-not","text":"What it is not","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERouter carving turns drawn shapes or a grey picture into G-code for a CNC router (or any machine that follows G-code). It does three things:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EV-carve\u003C/strong\u003E: closed outlines such as letters and logos are carved with a V-bit that follows the middle of each shape, going deeper where the shape is wider, so the walls of the cut meet the edge of the outline exactly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave\u003C/strong\u003E: lines and outlines are followed at a depth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERelief\u003C/strong\u003E: a grey picture becomes a 3D relief. White stays at the top and black is cut to the full depth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an add-on, not another app. It is free and off until you switch it on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERouter carving\u003C/span\u003E. Its tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarving\u003C/span\u003E, is then in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-v-carve\"\u003EYour first V-carve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw closed outlines, or open a drawing whose letters are outlines (text is not outlines: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:carving/carving-not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"addon:carving/carving-not-built-yet\"\u003Ewhat it does not do yet\u003C/a\u003E), or select the ones you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarving\u003C/span\u003E tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShapes\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole drawing\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelection\u003C/span\u003E. The line under it says how many closed shapes and open lines it found.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat to cut\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EV-carve\u003C/span\u003E. Set the \u003Cstrong\u003Ebit angle\u003C/strong\u003E (60° and 90° are usual) and the \u003Cstrong\u003Edeepest cut\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMachine\u003C/span\u003E, set the feeds, the spindle speed and the heights for your machine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake toolpaths\u003C/span\u003E. The preview shows the block as it would be cut, darker where deeper, and the program is checked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload G-code\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw toolpaths in the drawing\u003C/span\u003E to see them on the CARVE layers first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe commands \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECARVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECARVEDRAW\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECARVEGCODE\u003C/code\u003E do steps 5 and 6 with the settings in the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"always-air-cut-the-first-run\"\u003EAlways air-cut the first run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe program is a starting point. Different machines read G-code differently, so check the post-processor against your machine&#039;s manual, set the zero where your program expects it, and run it above the material first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is-not\"\u003EWhat it is not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does not drive your machine, simulate chip loads or spindle limits, or know your bit&#039;s real geometry. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:carving/carving-not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"addon:carving/carving-not-built-yet\"\u003Ewhat it does not do yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Router carving turns drawn shapes or a grey picture into G-code for a CNC router (or any machine that follows G-code). It does three things: V-carve: closed outlines such as letters and logos are carved with a V-bit that follows the middle of each shape, going deeper where the shape is wider, so the walls of the cut meet the edge of the outline exactly. Engrave: lines and outlines are followed at a depth. Relief: a grey picture becomes a 3D relief. White stays at the top and black is cut to the full depth. It is an add-on, not another app. It is free and off until you switch it on in Settings › General › Add-ons › Router carving. Its tab, Carving, is then in the right panel. Your first V-carve Draw closed outlines, or open a drawing whose letters are outlines (text is not outlines: see what it does not do yet), or select the ones you want. In the Carving tab, under Shapes, choose Whole drawing or Selection. The line under it says how many closed shapes and open lines it found. Under What to cut, choose V-carve. Set the bit angle (60° and 90° are usual) and the deepest cut. Under Machine, set the feeds, the spindle speed and the heights for your machine. Choose Make toolpaths. The preview shows the block as it would be cut, darker where deeper, and the program is checked. Download G-code, or Draw toolpaths in the drawing to see them on the CARVE layers first. The commands CARVE, CARVEDRAW and CARVEGCODE do steps 5 and 6 with the settings in the panel. Always air-cut the first run The program is a starting point. Different machines read G-code differently, so check the post-processor against your machine's manual, set the zero where your program expects it, and run it above the material first. What it is not It does not drive your machine, simulate chip loads or spindle limits, or know your bit's real geometry. See what it does not do yet."},{"slug":"carving-vcarve","scope":"addon:carving","title":"V-carving letters and logos","summary":"How the V-bit is moved, why the depth follows the width, what the flat floor and clearing options are, and how exact the result is.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["v-carve","vcarve","v bit","angle","depth","flat floor","clearing","medial axis","ridge","letters","logo","inlay"],"headings":[{"id":"how-the-cut-is-made","text":"How the cut is made","level":2},{"id":"deepest-cut-and-the-flat-floor","text":"Deepest cut and the flat floor","level":2},{"id":"how-exact-it-is","text":"How exact it is","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-cut-is-made\"\u003EHow the cut is made\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA V-bit cuts a groove as wide as it is deep: at the angle of the bit, a depth of 3 mm with a 60° bit is a groove 3.46 mm wide at the top. So a wide part of a shape needs a deep cut and a narrow part a shallow one. Router carving follows the line that is equally far from the edges (the middle of each stroke), with the tip as deep as the width there needs. The walls of the cut then meet the edge of the outline all the way round, and corners come to a point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShapes are read by the even-odd rule: a closed shape inside another is a hole (the middle of an O).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"deepest-cut-and-the-flat-floor\"\u003EDeepest cut and the flat floor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou set the \u003Cstrong\u003Edeepest cut\u003C/strong\u003E. Where a shape is wider than twice that depth times the tangent of half the bit angle, the V-bit alone would have to go deeper than allowed, so the floor is \u003Cstrong\u003Eflat\u003C/strong\u003E at the deepest cut and the walls are cut at full depth. The result panel tells you how wide that is. Choose what happens to the floor:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClear with a flat mill\u003C/strong\u003E: a flat end mill (give its diameter and stepover) clears the floor in passes from the walls inward, then the V-bit trims the strip next to the walls. This is the usual way and the fastest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClear with the V-bit\u003C/strong\u003E: the V-bit cuts the floor itself in many passes. It works, but a V-bit leaves a ridge between passes (you choose how high, default 0.15 mm), so it takes many passes and is refused beyond 400.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeave it\u003C/strong\u003E: only the walls are cut and the floor is left standing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe flat mill and the V-bit are two operations with a tool change between: the program asks for the tool, or on GRBL pauses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-exact-it-is\"\u003EHow exact it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tests cut the toolpaths into a simulated block and compare it with the ideal V-carve. On letters, rings, rectangles and stars, with a 60° and a 90° bit, nothing is cut deeper than about one simulator cell, and under half a percent of a shape is left more than 0.3 mm shallower than ideal. That is the model of the cutter&#039;s shape only: it does not know your machine&#039;s accuracy, the bit&#039;s tip radius (a real V-bit has a small flat on its tip) or chatter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery thin features, narrower than about two grid cells (0.1 mm by default), are not carved.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How the cut is made A V-bit cuts a groove as wide as it is deep: at the angle of the bit, a depth of 3 mm with a 60° bit is a groove 3.46 mm wide at the top. So a wide part of a shape needs a deep cut and a narrow part a shallow one. Router carving follows the line that is equally far from the edges (the middle of each stroke), with the tip as deep as the width there needs. The walls of the cut then meet the edge of the outline all the way round, and corners come to a point. Shapes are read by the even-odd rule: a closed shape inside another is a hole (the middle of an O). Deepest cut and the flat floor You set the deepest cut. Where a shape is wider than twice that depth times the tangent of half the bit angle, the V-bit alone would have to go deeper than allowed, so the floor is flat at the deepest cut and the walls are cut at full depth. The result panel tells you how wide that is. Choose what happens to the floor: Clear with a flat mill: a flat end mill (give its diameter and stepover) clears the floor in passes from the walls inward, then the V-bit trims the strip next to the walls. This is the usual way and the fastest. Clear with the V-bit: the V-bit cuts the floor itself in many passes. It works, but a V-bit leaves a ridge between passes (you choose how high, default 0.15 mm), so it takes many passes and is refused beyond 400. Leave it: only the walls are cut and the floor is left standing. The flat mill and the V-bit are two operations with a tool change between: the program asks for the tool, or on GRBL pauses. How exact it is The tests cut the toolpaths into a simulated block and compare it with the ideal V-carve. On letters, rings, rectangles and stars, with a 60° and a 90° bit, nothing is cut deeper than about one simulator cell, and under half a percent of a shape is left more than 0.3 mm shallower than ideal. That is the model of the cutter's shape only: it does not know your machine's accuracy, the bit's tip radius (a real V-bit has a small flat on its tip) or chatter. Very thin features, narrower than about two grid cells (0.1 mm by default), are not carved."},{"slug":"carving-relief","scope":"addon:carving","title":"Carving a relief from a picture, and engraving","summary":"Turn a grey picture into a 3D relief with a ball, flat or V cutter and a roughing pass, or follow lines at a depth.","section":"tasks","order":3,"keywords":["relief","depth map","heightmap","grey picture","3d carving","ball nose","roughing","stepover","scallop","engraving","engrave"],"headings":[{"id":"a-relief-from-a-picture","text":"A relief from a picture","level":2},{"id":"it-does-not-cut-into-the-picture","text":"It does not cut into the picture","level":2},{"id":"engraving","text":"Engraving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"a-relief-from-a-picture\"\u003EA relief from a picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat to cut\u003C/span\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERelief\u003C/span\u003E and pick a picture. A grey picture works best: white stays at the top of the material and black is cut to the full depth (tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlack is the top\u003C/span\u003E to turn that round).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cstrong\u003Ewidth\u003C/strong\u003E of the relief (the height follows from the picture) and the \u003Cstrong\u003Edepth\u003C/strong\u003E, and where its lower left corner goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the cutter: ball nose (the usual finishing bit), flat end mill, or V-bit. A ball nose leaves a small ridge between lines; the result panel tells you how high.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003ERough first\u003C/strong\u003E ticked to clear most of the material with a flat mill in layers first, leaving a little (default 0.3 mm) for the finishing pass.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake toolpaths\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is resampled to at most 1200 pixels on its long side. The size of a pixel on the relief limits how fine the lines can be: a V-bit needs fine lines, so use a smaller relief or a larger picture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"it-does-not-cut-into-the-picture\"\u003EIt does not cut into the picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cutter is dropped onto the surface: at every point its tip goes as low as it can without the body of the cutter touching the relief. The tests cut the paths into a simulated block and check that finishing never goes below the picture, with a ball nose, a flat mill and a V-bit. A round tool cannot reach a corner narrower than itself, so a deep corner is left rounded, and the border of the picture within a tool radius is left as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"engraving\"\u003EEngraving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngrave\u003C/span\u003E to follow lines and outlines at a depth, in passes of at most the depth you set. Open lines and closed shapes are both followed. A V-bit&#039;s line is as wide at the top as it is deep: the result panel gives the width.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A relief from a picture Under What to cut choose Relief and pick a picture. A grey picture works best: white stays at the top of the material and black is cut to the full depth (tick Black is the top to turn that round). Set the width of the relief (the height follows from the picture) and the depth, and where its lower left corner goes. Choose the cutter: ball nose (the usual finishing bit), flat end mill, or V-bit. A ball nose leaves a small ridge between lines; the result panel tells you how high. Leave Rough first ticked to clear most of the material with a flat mill in layers first, leaving a little (default 0.3 mm) for the finishing pass. Make toolpaths. The picture is resampled to at most 1200 pixels on its long side. The size of a pixel on the relief limits how fine the lines can be: a V-bit needs fine lines, so use a smaller relief or a larger picture. It does not cut into the picture The cutter is dropped onto the surface: at every point its tip goes as low as it can without the body of the cutter touching the relief. The tests cut the paths into a simulated block and check that finishing never goes below the picture, with a ball nose, a flat mill and a V-bit. A round tool cannot reach a corner narrower than itself, so a deep corner is left rounded, and the border of the picture within a tool radius is left as it is. Engraving Choose Engrave to follow lines and outlines at a depth, in passes of at most the depth you set. Open lines and closed shapes are both followed. A V-bit's line is as wide at the top as it is deep: the result panel gives the width."},{"slug":"carving-post-check","scope":"addon:carving","title":"The post-processor and the program check","summary":"Change the text the G-code has around the cuts to suit your machine, and what the check of the program looks for.","section":"tasks","order":4,"keywords":["post-processor","post processor","g-code","grbl","linuxcnc","mach3","fanuc","header","footer","tool change","spindle","check","simulation"],"headings":[{"id":"the-post-processor","text":"The post-processor","level":2},{"id":"the-check","text":"The check","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-know","text":"What it does not know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-post-processor\"\u003EThe post-processor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA post-processor is the text a machine needs around the cuts: the start of the program, what to do for a tool change, how to start and stop the spindle, and the end. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPost-processor\u003C/span\u003E choose a dialect: GRBL (hobby routers and lasers), LinuxCNC, Mach3 or Fanuc style. These are generic: check them against your machine&#039;s manual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit the post-processor\u003C/span\u003E to change the lines. {rpm} {tool} {name} {safe} {feed} {unit} are filled in. Your changes are kept for that dialect, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the built-in\u003C/span\u003E drops them. You can also number the blocks (N10, N20 …).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGRBL has no tool change command, so the program stops with a pause that names the tool; resume once you have changed it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-check\"\u003EThe check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery program is read back and checked for what would crash or waste a job: a cut before any feed rate, a cut below the top with the spindle off, a fast move across the work below its top, a move outside the table (give the table size), a cut deeper than the material, no millimetres or inches declared, and no program end. To check a program from somewhere else, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck a G-code file\u003C/span\u003E and pick the file (.gcode, .nc, .ngc, .tap, .txt): its problems are listed the same way. An AI assistant can run the same check on any program through the add-on&#039;s engine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-know\"\u003EWhat it does not know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe check does not know your machine&#039;s limits, your clamps or your bit&#039;s real length. It is a second pair of eyes, not a guarantee.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The post-processor A post-processor is the text a machine needs around the cuts: the start of the program, what to do for a tool change, how to start and stop the spindle, and the end. Under Post-processor choose a dialect: GRBL (hobby routers and lasers), LinuxCNC, Mach3 or Fanuc style. These are generic: check them against your machine's manual. Open Edit the post-processor to change the lines. {rpm} {tool} {name} {safe} {feed} {unit} are filled in. Your changes are kept for that dialect, and Back to the built-in drops them. You can also number the blocks (N10, N20 …). GRBL has no tool change command, so the program stops with a pause that names the tool; resume once you have changed it. The check Every program is read back and checked for what would crash or waste a job: a cut before any feed rate, a cut below the top with the spindle off, a fast move across the work below its top, a move outside the table (give the table size), a cut deeper than the material, no millimetres or inches declared, and no program end. To check a program from somewhere else, choose Check a G-code file and pick the file (.gcode, .nc, .ngc, .tap, .txt): its problems are listed the same way. An AI assistant can run the same check on any program through the add-on's engine. What it does not know The check does not know your machine's limits, your clamps or your bit's real length. It is a second pair of eyes, not a guarantee."},{"slug":"carving-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:carving","title":"Router carving: what it does not do yet","summary":"The things people ask a CAM program for that Router carving does not do yet, so you know before you start.","section":"tasks","order":5,"keywords":["not built","limits","5 axis","lathe","tool library","simulation","nesting","text","fonts"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built","text":"Not built","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built\"\u003ENot built\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELetters from text.\u003C/strong\u003E Router carving carves outlines, and CadCraft has no command to turn text into outlines yet, so text in a drawing is not carved. Bring letters in as outlines (for example a DXF or SVG where the text was converted to curves in the program that made it) or draw them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicture to outline.\u003C/strong\u003E It does not trace a picture into vector shapes; for a picture use a relief.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA tool library.\u003C/strong\u003E You type the bit each time; the settings are kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReal machine limits.\u003C/strong\u003E The preview and the check model the cutter&#039;s shape and the program&#039;s text, not your machine&#039;s acceleration, spindle power or the tip radius of a real V-bit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo-sided work, 4 and 5 axis, lathe.\u003C/strong\u003E Not built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETabs and holding down, lead-in and lead-out arcs, ramping.\u003C/strong\u003E Cuts plunge at the plunge feed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs in the G-code.\u003C/strong\u003E Paths are short straight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther apps.\u003C/strong\u003E It is in CadCraft. PartCraft, ModelCraft and SuperCad Studio come later through the same engine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETell us what you need first.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Not built Letters from text. Router carving carves outlines, and CadCraft has no command to turn text into outlines yet, so text in a drawing is not carved. Bring letters in as outlines (for example a DXF or SVG where the text was converted to curves in the program that made it) or draw them. Picture to outline. It does not trace a picture into vector shapes; for a picture use a relief. A tool library. You type the bit each time; the settings are kept. Real machine limits. The preview and the check model the cutter's shape and the program's text, not your machine's acceleration, spindle power or the tip radius of a real V-bit. Two-sided work, 4 and 5 axis, lathe. Not built. Tabs and holding down, lead-in and lead-out arcs, ramping. Cuts plunge at the plunge feed. Arcs in the G-code. Paths are short straight lines. Other apps. It is in CadCraft. PartCraft, ModelCraft and SuperCad Studio come later through the same engine. Tell us what you need first."}]};
